  <p>The long-term trajectory of crude oil prices reveals a pronounced structural shift beginning with the 1973 Oil Embargo, which ended an era of extraordinary price stability ($1.21–$2.70/bbl during 1970–1973). Subsequent decades have been defined by recurring boom-bust cycles tied to geopolitical crises, supply disruptions, demand shocks, and technological change. The 2000s and 2010s stand out as the highest-price decades, driven by surging emerging-market demand and supply constraints, while the 2020s have been marked by extreme pandemic-induced volatility followed by geopolitical price spikes and subsequent normalization.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Crude oil prices have demonstrated a compound annual growth rate of <strong>{cagr:.2f}%</strong> over the full observation period. However, this headline figure masks extreme cyclicality — prices have swung from single digits to over $130/bbl, with multiple boom-bust cycles producing drawdowns exceeding 50% within 6–12 months.
  </div>
</section>

