<section id="outlook-and-conclusions">
  <h2>6. Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>
  <p>The 2020s have already proven to be one of the most volatile decades in the history of crude oil pricing. Beginning with the unprecedented COVID-19 demand shock in April 2020 (${data_payload["key_events"][12]["price"]:,.2f}/bbl), prices recovered rapidly through 2021–2022, spiking to ${data_payload["key_events"][13]["price"]:,.2f}/bbl in March 2022 following Russia's invasion of Ukraine. The subsequent period (2023–2025) saw a gradual normalization toward the $60–$85 range, with the latest observation at <strong>${last_price:,.2f}/bbl</strong> in {last_date_str[:7].replace("-", " ")}.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Structural Observation:</strong> The decade average for the 2020s currently stands at <strong>${decade_stats['2020']['avg']:,.2f}/bbl</strong> with a standard deviation of <strong>${decade_stats['2020']['std']:,.2f}</strong> — already the second-highest volatility decade on record, trailing only the 2000s (std: ${decade_stats['2000']['std']:,.2f}). This reflects the compounding effects of pandemic disruption, geopolitical conflict, energy transition uncertainty, and persistent supply-demand imbalances.
  </div>

  <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Structural breaks define the market.</strong> Crude oil prices do not follow a smooth trend — they are shaped by discrete geopolitical and economic shocks that create new pricing regimes. The 1973 embargo, 1986 price collapse, 2008 financial crisis, 2014 shale revolution, and 2020 pandemic each represent structural breaks visible in the data.</li>
    <li><strong>Volatility clusters around crises.</strong> The largest month-over-month moves are concentrated around identifiable events: the 1973–74 embargo (+216% in a single month), the 1986 price war collapse (−35% MoM), the 2008 GFC (−45% in a single month), and the 2020 COVID shock (−40% MoM).</li>
    <li><strong>The long-term uptrend is real but driven by discrete jumps.</strong> The {cagr:.2f}% CAGR over {round(years_elapsed, 1)} years reflects step-function increases concentrated in the 1973–1980 and 2004–2008 periods. Prices were range-bound for extended periods between these structural shifts.</li>
    <li><strong>Recent data suggests a new equilibrium.</strong> Following the extreme volatility of 2020–2022, prices in 2023–2026 appear to be consolidating in a range roughly bounded by $55–$90/bbl, with the March 2026 observation of $87.84 suggesting possible renewed upward pressure.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Data Caveats</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All prices are in <strong>nominal US dollars</strong> and are not adjusted for inflation. Real (CPI-deflated) prices would show lower values in recent decades and higher relative values in the 1970s.</li>
    <li>Data points from <strong>2024 onwards</strong> may include forecasts or projections rather than exclusively observed market prices, per the dataset documentation.</li>
    <li>The dataset uses a <strong>single benchmark price series</strong> and may not reflect grade-specific or region-specific pricing differentials.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

