<body>
<div class="report-container">

<!-- ═══════════ HEADER ═══════════ -->
<header class="report-header">
  <h1>Global Crude Oil Price Trends: A Historical Analysis (1970–2026)</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive trend analysis of monthly crude oil prices spanning over five decades, identifying key price regimes, shocks, and long-term patterns.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span>Dataset: {n} monthly observations</span>
    <span>Range: {first_date_str} – {last_date_str}</span>
    <span>Analysis Date: May 2026</span>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW ═══════════ -->
<section id="executive-overview">
  <h2>1. Executive Overview</h2>
  <p>This report analyzes <strong>{n} monthly crude oil price observations</strong> spanning from <strong>{first_date_str} to {last_date_str}</strong>. Over this {round(years_elapsed, 1)}-year period, nominal crude oil prices have traversed an extraordinary range — from a low of <strong>${min_price:.2f}</strong> per barrel in {min_date[:7].replace("-", " ")} to an all-time high of <strong>${max_price:.2f}</strong> in {max_date[:7].replace("-", " ")}.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">${overall_range:,.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Overall Price Range (USD/bbl)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{cagr:,.2f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">CAGR ({first_date_str[:4]}–{last_date_str[:4]})</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">${max_mom_abs:,.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Max MoM Absolute Change</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">{highest_avg_decade[0]}s</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Decade with Highest Avg Price (${highest_avg_decade[1]["avg"]:,.2f})</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">${overall_std:,.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Overall Std Deviation</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The long-term trajectory of crude oil prices reveals a pronounced structural shift beginning with the 1973 Oil Embargo, which ended an era of extraordinary price stability ($1.21–$2.70/bbl during 1970–1973). Subsequent decades have been defined by recurring boom-bust cycles tied to geopolitical crises, supply disruptions, demand shocks, and technological change. The 2000s and 2010s stand out as the highest-price decades, driven by surging emerging-market demand and supply constraints, while the 2020s have been marked by extreme pandemic-induced volatility followed by geopolitical price spikes and subsequent normalization.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> Crude oil prices have demonstrated a compound annual growth rate of <strong>{cagr:.2f}%</strong> over the full observation period. However, this headline figure masks extreme cyclicality — prices have swung from single digits to over $130/bbl, with multiple boom-bust cycles producing drawdowns exceeding 50% within 6–12 months.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: LONG-TERM PRICE TRAJECTORY ═══════════ -->
<section id="long-term-trend">
  <h2>2. Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
  <p>The full time series below captures every major inflection point in the global crude oil market over the past five decades. Key structural breaks are annotated to highlight the historical events that shaped pricing regimes.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrapper chart-wrapper-lg">
    <canvas id="chartFullSeries"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:16px;">The chart reveals four distinct price regimes: <strong>(1) Pre-Embargo Stability (1970–1973)</strong> with prices locked near $1–$2/bbl; <strong>(2) The OPEC Era (1974–1985)</strong> characterized by structurally higher but volatile prices following the 1973 and 1979 oil shocks; <strong>(3) The Moderation Period (1986–2003)</strong> with prices generally oscillating between $10–$35/bbl, punctuated by the 1990 Gulf War spike and the 1998 Asian Financial Crisis trough; and <strong>(4) The Super-Cycle &amp; Volatility Era (2004–2026)</strong> featuring the dramatic run-up to the 2008 all-time high, the shale revolution, COVID-19 collapse, and post-pandemic recovery.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 3: DECADE-BY-DECADE BREAKDOWN ═══════════ -->
<section id="decade-by-decade">
  <h2>3. Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <p>Grouping prices by decade reveals the structural evolution of the crude oil market. The table and chart below compare average prices, volatility (standard deviation), and price ranges across each decade.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrapper chart-wrapper-md">
    <canvas id="chartDecadeBars"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-wrapper chart-wrapper-md" style="margin-top:24px;">
    <canvas id="chartAnnualAvg"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table id="decadeTable"></table>

  <div class="decade-timeline" style="margin-top:16px;">
    <span class="decade-pill" style="background:var(--decade-1970);">1970s: Post-Embargo Shock</span>
    <span class="decade-pill" style="background:var(--decade-1980);">1980s: Glut &amp; Collapse</span>
    <span class="decade-pill" style="background:var(--decade-1990);">1990s: Relative Stability</span>
    <span class="decade-pill" style="background:var(--decade-2000);">2000s: Super-Cycle</span>
    <span class="decade-pill" style="background:var(--decade-2010);">2010s: Shale &amp; Swings</span>
    <span class="decade-pill" style="background:var(--decade-2020);">2020s: Pandemic &amp; Recovery</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 4: VOLATILITY & MAJOR SHOCKS ═══════════ -->
<section id="volatility-and-shocks">
  <h2>4. Price Volatility &amp; Major Shocks</h2>
  <p>Crude oil is among the most volatile major commodities. This section quantifies the periods of extreme price movement and maps them to underlying events.</p>

  <h3>4.1 Month-over-Month Price Changes</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrapper chart-wrapper-lg">
    <canvas id="chartMoM"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>4.2 Top 10 Largest Monthly Moves (Absolute)</h3>
  <table id="topMomAbsTable"></table>

  <h3>4.3 Top 10 Largest Monthly Moves (Percentage)</h3>
  <table id="topMomPctTable"></table>

  <h3>4.4 Year-over-Year Price Changes</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrapper chart-wrapper-lg">
    <canvas id="chartYoY"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>4.5 Key Historical Events &amp; Price Impact</h3>
  <table id="eventsTable">
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th>Price (USD/bbl)</th><th>Classification</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody></tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 5: STATISTICAL SUMMARY ═══════════ -->
<section id="statistical-summary">
  <h2>5. Statistical Summary &amp; Distribution</h2>
  <p>Descriptive statistics and distribution analysis of monthly crude oil prices across the entire 1970–2026 dataset.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>5.1 Descriptive Statistics</h3>
      <table>
        <tr><td>Mean</td><td class="number"><strong>${mean_price:,.2f}</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Median (P50)</td><td class="number"><strong>${p50:,.2f}</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Standard Deviation</td><td class="number"><strong>${overall_std:,.2f}</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Minimum</td><td class="number"><strong>${min_price:,.2f}</strong> ({min_date[:7].replace("-", " ")})</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maximum</td><td class="number"><strong>${max_price:,.2f}</strong> ({max_date[:7].replace("-", " ")})</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Range</td><td class="number"><strong>${overall_range:,.2f}</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>10th Percentile</td><td class="number">${p10:,.2f}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>25th Percentile</td><td class="number">${p25:,.2f}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>75th Percentile</td><td class="number">${p75:,.2f}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>90th Percentile</td><td class="number">${p90:,.2f}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Skewness (mean &gt; median)</td><td class="number">Positive (right-skewed)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Observations</td><td class="number"><strong>{n}</strong></td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>5.2 Price Distribution</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrapper chart-wrapper-sm">
        <canvas id="chartHistogram"></canvas>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:12px;">The distribution is strongly right-skewed: the mean (${mean_price:,.2f}) exceeds the median (${p50:,.2f}), reflecting the influence of extreme high-price episodes (particularly 2008 and 2011–2014). Over 50% of monthly observations fall below $35/bbl, but the long right tail extends past $130/bbl.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>5.3 Price Regime Summary</h3>
  <p>The coefficient of variation (Std Dev / Mean) is <strong>{round(overall_std/mean_price*100, 1)}%</strong>, underscoring the extreme relative dispersion characteristic of commodity prices driven by supply shocks and demand cycles. The interquartile range (P25–P75) spans <strong>${p25:,.2f}–${p75:,.2f}</strong>, meaning the middle 50% of monthly prices fall within a relatively narrow band despite the dramatic extremes at both tails.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: OUTLOOK & CONCLUSIONS ═══════════ -->
<section id="outlook-and-conclusions">
  <h2>6. Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>
  <p>The 2020s have already proven to be one of the most volatile decades in the history of crude oil pricing. Beginning with the unprecedented COVID-19 demand shock in April 2020 (${data_payload["key_events"][12]["price"]:,.2f}/bbl), prices recovered rapidly through 2021–2022, spiking to ${data_payload["key_events"][13]["price"]:,.2f}/bbl in March 2022 following Russia's invasion of Ukraine. The subsequent period (2023–2025) saw a gradual normalization toward the $60–$85 range, with the latest observation at <strong>${last_price:,.2f}/bbl</strong> in {last_date_str[:7].replace("-", " ")}.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Structural Observation:</strong> The decade average for the 2020s currently stands at <strong>${decade_stats['2020']['avg']:,.2f}/bbl</strong> with a standard deviation of <strong>${decade_stats['2020']['std']:,.2f}</strong> — already the second-highest volatility decade on record, trailing only the 2000s (std: ${decade_stats['2000']['std']:,.2f}). This reflects the compounding effects of pandemic disruption, geopolitical conflict, energy transition uncertainty, and persistent supply-demand imbalances.
  </div>

  <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Structural breaks define the market.</strong> Crude oil prices do not follow a smooth trend — they are shaped by discrete geopolitical and economic shocks that create new pricing regimes. The 1973 embargo, 1986 price collapse, 2008 financial crisis, 2014 shale revolution, and 2020 pandemic each represent structural breaks visible in the data.</li>
    <li><strong>Volatility clusters around crises.</strong> The largest month-over-month moves are concentrated around identifiable events: the 1973–74 embargo (+216% in a single month), the 1986 price war collapse (−35% MoM), the 2008 GFC (−45% in a single month), and the 2020 COVID shock (−40% MoM).</li>
    <li><strong>The long-term uptrend is real but driven by discrete jumps.</strong> The {cagr:.2f}% CAGR over {round(years_elapsed, 1)} years reflects step-function increases concentrated in the 1973–1980 and 2004–2008 periods. Prices were range-bound for extended periods between these structural shifts.</li>
    <li><strong>Recent data suggests a new equilibrium.</strong> Following the extreme volatility of 2020–2022, prices in 2023–2026 appear to be consolidating in a range roughly bounded by $55–$90/bbl, with the March 2026 observation of $87.84 suggesting possible renewed upward pressure.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Data Caveats</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All prices are in <strong>nominal US dollars</strong> and are not adjusted for inflation. Real (CPI-deflated) prices would show lower values in recent decades and higher relative values in the 1970s.</li>
    <li>Data points from <strong>2024 onwards</strong> may include forecasts or projections rather than exclusively observed market prices, per the dataset documentation.</li>
    <li>The dataset uses a <strong>single benchmark price series</strong> and may not reflect grade-specific or region-specific pricing differentials.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<div class="footer">
  <p>Data source: Global Fuel Price Trends (1970–2026) | Analysis generated May 2026</p>
</div>

